From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] hvf: a line in qemu.log when a vCPU stands still (Mac memory, no core)

What: when macOS runs out of free memory, a vCPU thread can stay blocked in
hv_vcpu_run for seconds (a stage-2 page fault waiting while macOS decompresses
or swaps in guest RAM, or for a free page) while the other vCPUs run on. The
guest then logs RCU stalls, its watchdogs fire, and nothing on the Mac side said
why. Each vCPU thread now counts its hv_vcpu_run calls (one relaxed store before
and one after the call, odd while in the guest). The stall watchdog's thread,
when it wakes anyway (every 2 s at most), reads every vCPU thread's CPU time and
Mach state with thread_info(); a vCPU that is still in the same call, got no
CPU time since, and is uninterruptible (blocked in macOS's kernel) or runnable
(no Mac core) stands still. While one stands, the watchdog looks every 250 ms;
a vCPU idle in the guest's WFI (waiting) never counts. Blocked in the kernel is
put down to memory only when the Mac was paging at that time: from the second
look before the episode was seen to its end, macOS's compressor and swap moved
16 MB/s or more, swapped 1 MB or more, or the pressure level was warn or
critical (host_statistics64 and one sysctl each round; a healthy idle MacBook
Air moves about 0.03 MB/s, the swapping mini about 4 GB/s). Otherwise the line
says only "blocked in macOS's kernel" (another macOS version may block a vCPU
there for other reasons) and the app sends no note. One line per episode of
1 s or more (and one at 10 s, then every 30 s while it lasts); for example
(the format, not a measurement):
  OmacVM: stall: vCPUs 1, 2, 3, 8 stood still for about 58 s, blocked in
  macOS's kernel waiting for memory (Mac: compressor and swap 900 MB/s, 0.3 GB
  free, pressure normal; the VM holds 51 GB, 20 GB of it compressed by macOS)
  OmacVM: stall: vCPU 3 stood still for about 2.4 s, blocked in macOS's kernel
  (the Mac was not paging: compressor and swap 0 MB/s, pressure normal)
  OmacVM: stall: vCPU 5 waited 2.1 s for a Mac core (the Mac's CPUs are busy:
  load average 30)
vCPU numbers are the guest's CPU numbers. When it started is taken from the
vCPU's CPU rate before (a vCPU mostly idle: the latest it can be). Limits (no
timer of its own, 2 s between wakes): a vCPU blocked in the kernel is always
found from 2.25 s, from 3.25 s when it ran under 10 % of a core before; one
without a Mac core from 4 s; shorter ones can be missed. A time is never more
than 0.15 s longer than the stall, within 0.25 s for a vCPU that ran 10 % or
more before, up to 2.1 s short for one that ran less (1000 random stalls each
in the test; a 20 000-stall sweep missed none longer than 2.21 / 2.71 / 3.91 s). No line while the VM is not running,
and none for a look that came more than 1 s late or after the Mac slept (App
Nap, QEMU stopped): times are CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW, which stops while the Mac sleeps.
A watchdog wake more than 1 s late (not a Mac that slept) gets its own line, at
most one per 10 minutes, the late wakes between counted in the next one:
  OmacVM: stall: the stall watchdog woke 3.2 s late: QEMU got no Mac core or
  waited in macOS that long; vCPU stalls in that time are not counted
logs/vm-stalls ($OMACVM_LOGS/vm-stalls, key=value lines, all zeros from the
watchdog's start, replaced in one rename, no fsync): only the watchdog's thread
writes it, after the status lock is let go; the main loop, which may hold the
BQL, only records its own stalls in memory. Keys: stalls, longest_ms,
longest_cause, last_ms, last_cause (memory, kernel, cpu), last_vcpus, last_at, last_vm_mb,
last_free_mb, last_compressed_mb, and the main loop's stalls of 2 s or more in
a GPU run: gpu_stalls, gpu_longest_ms, gpu_last_where (the Mac's call and the
frame of QEMU or virglrenderer that made it), and outside one, with the BQL
held or waited for, so the vCPUs waited too: main_stalls, main_longest_ms,
main_last_where (e.g. "close (from qemu_memfd_free)": 2.2 s on the swapping
mini). OmacVM.app and omacvm check read it.
OMACVM_STALL_WATCHDOG=0 turns it off with the watchdog (no thread_info at all).
Why: the user's VM froze on 2026-10-28 (RCU stall of ~60 s on CPUs 1, 2, 3, 8,
"no word from the Mac for 15 s"); on the Mac mini at critical memory pressure
the same showed up as vCPU threads uninterruptible in hv_vcpu_run for 1.2-2.4 s
with the BQL free (tracks/vm-stall.md). The line names that at once.
Upstream: OmacVM only.
Test: app/runtime/Tests/vcpu/test-vcpu-stall.sh (the logic and text in
include/qemu/omacvm-stall.h against simulated vCPU threads: the mini's stall,
the user's 60 s as a shape, 3 x 1000 random stalls at the limits above, paging
noise, idle, late wakes, pauses; paging or not; the status between threads;
build time and CI); the mini's repro row (work/vm-stall/kit/run-cpu.sh).

